nst the report data surface produced by the reporting backend (stable API / data contract). Consuming Implementation is serial on the producer. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Documentation describes the report semantics and export integration that the reporting backend delivers. |\n| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Documentation covers the administrator-facing behavior including the Dashboard view, so it is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic | Notes |\n|-----|----------|------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | E002 | Per-project GPU-hour reports over selectable time ranges, computed from metering data. |\n| HLR-2 | P1 | E002 (bundled) — CSV export and the stable API are export modes of the same report surface over the same metering data; a split would build the same data structure and API endpoint twice and add an artificial serialization edge, so the P1 stays bundled with the P0 report work. | |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | E003 | Dashboard admin view: current-month consumption against each project's Kueue quota. |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | E004 | Documentation of report semantics and export integration. Docs-authoring priority derived from strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception. |\n\nAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Missing strategy sections.** The strategy provides no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, no Non-Functional Requirements, and no per-component change descriptions in the Affected Components list. Team assignments were inferred from `.context/architecture-context/` owning teams (RHOAI Dashboard for the Dashboard component, Distributed Workloads for the Distributed Workloads component). The documentation epic is assigned to a docs team (\"RHOAI Docs\") as an assumption — no team information exists in the strategy; verify before scheduling.\n2. **External timing risk (not an AC).** The metering data schema finalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands. Tier 2 work (E002, E003, E004) is precondition-gated on that data becoming available; this is a schedule risk recorded in epic Risks/Notes, not an acceptance criterion. E001 is the Tier 1 risk-reduction activity that pins the consumption contract early.\n3. **Dashboard consumption interface.** The Dashboard architecture context states the Dashboard talks to the Kubernetes API only and owns no data plane, while the strategy mandates a stable report API for finance tooling. Whether the Dashboard admin view consumes report data via that API or via a Kubernetes surface is an implementation decision for the Dashboard team; it does not change the epic structure. Flagged for the implementing team.\n4. **Priority note.** HLR-2 (P1) is deliberately bundled into the P0 report epic (see traceability matrix row for the rationale). No other priority inversions detected.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe external dependency (RHAISTRAT-9990 metering data, uncertain landing timing) admits a tiered split:\n\n- **Tier 1 (independent, risk-reduction):** E001 — the metering data consumption contract Investigation. It can start immediately against the in-flight schema work, de-risks the report implementation, and does not require RHAISTRAT-9990 to have landed.\n- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated):** E002, E003, E004 — all report computation, Dashboard presentation, and documentation work that consumes the metering data. These are gated by E001's contract and, as a schedule precondition, by RHAISTRAT-9990 delivering the metering data.\n\nNo separate epic is created for the external dependency itself (Rule 21, third party resolves): it is modeled as a precondition with tiered delivery.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":28223,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8ab43a0e5f0ec