{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-5e5e79b3-dcfc-45b6-b874-fb2fa3da7b12","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-88cb8f4b58f93eba","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9018-0anxspqq/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9018-E001.md","content":"# Pin the metering data consumption contract for GPU chargeback reporting\n\n## Description\n\nThis Investigation resolves the strategy's open question: the per-project GPU-hour metering data schema is owned by the in-flight GPU usage metering strategy (RHAISTRAT-9990) and is not final until that strategy lands. Before the chargeback reporting implementation starts, this epic pins down the exact consumption contract the reporting builds against, validates that the metering data can support the required reports, and agrees a fallback approach with the metering strategy owners if the schema shifts before landing. It produces no production code — its deliverable is an agreed, validated data consumption contract plus a recorded fallback.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Enumerate the concrete schema questions the chargeback reporting needs answered: per-project GPU-hour field structure, project identity mapping, time-range queryability, and data retention/availability characteristics.\n- Review the in-flight metering data schema from the GPU usage metering strategy against those questions.\n- Validate that per-project GPU-hour reports over selectable time ranges (the core reporting requirement) can be computed from the schema — including feasibility checks against draft or sample metering data where available.\n- Agree the consumption contract with the owners of the metering strategy and record their sign-off.\n- Define and document a fallback adaptation approach for the report implementation if the schema changes between contract agreement and the metering strategy landing.\n- Explicitly exclude re-implementing or modifying the metering collection pipeline itself.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A consumption contract document exists that enumerates every schema element the reporting depends on (per-project GPU-hour fields, project identity, time-range query surface, retention) with the metering strategy owners' agreement recorded.\n- Report feasibility over selectable time ranges is validated against the in-flight schema (using draft or sample data where available), with any gaps documented and raised to the metering strategy owners.\n- A fallback adaptation approach is documented for the case where the schema shifts before the metering strategy lands.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- No HLR is directly covered by this Investigation. It resolves the strategy's open question that gates the implementation of HLR-1 (P0), HLR-2 (P1), and HLR-3 (P1); its outcome determines the data-consumption approach of the report computation, the export surfaces, and the Dashboard view built on top of them.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- The final schema is owned by a separate in-flight strategy; this Investigation pins a contract and a fallback but cannot force the schema to be final. Residual timing risk is carried by the downstream implementation epics as a schedule precondition, not as an acceptance criterion here.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":31003,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8ab43a0e5f0ec23d","agent_id":"a5db3396ff8040630","session_id":"664fa4a8-d231-49b0-afe7-236ef7303dfd","uuid":"6c09dc95-e0d9-4beb-acbf-039cbc7855c9","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:11:01.557Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9018"}
