{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b1befa38eac7f86f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9018 (GPU chargeback reporting for platform administrators)\n11\t\n12\t## Epic List\n13\t\n14\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n15\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n16\t| E001 | Pin the metering data consumption contract for GPU chargeback reporting | Investigation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n17\t| E002 | Per-project GPU-hour consumption reports with stable API and CSV export | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n18\t| E003 | Dashboard admin view of current-month GPU consumption against Kueue quota | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n19\t| E004 | Documentation for GPU chargeback report semantics and export integration | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Docs | P1 |\n20\t\n21\t## Dependency DAG\n22\t\n23\t```mermaid\n24\tgraph TD\n25\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: metering data consumption contract\"] --> E002[\"E002 Report computation, stable API, CSV export\"]\n26\t    E002 --> E003[\"E003 Dashboard admin view\"]\n27\t    E002 --> E004[\"E004 Report and export documentation\"]\n28\t    E003 --> E004\n29\t```\n30\t\n31\t## DAG Justification\n32\t\n33\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n34\t|------|------|-----------|\n35\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The metering data schema is explicitly flagged as an open question and is owned by the in-flight RHAISTRAT-9990 strategy. Its final shape determines what the report computation builds against, so the Investigation blocks the report Implementation. True gate: `rewrite` — if the schema shifts before landing, the report implementation's data-consumption approach changes. |\n36\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 12/13 (framework → consumer) | The Dashboard admin view builds against the report data surface produced by the reporting backend (stable API / data contract). Consuming Implementation is serial on the producer. |\n37\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Documentation describes the report semantics and export integration that the reporting backend delivers. |\n38\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Documentation covers the administrator-facing behavior including the Dashboard view, so it is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy. |\n39\t\n40\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n41\t\n42\t| HLR | Priority | Epic | Notes |\n43\t|-----|----------|------|-------|\n44\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E002 | Per-project GPU-hour reports over selectable time ranges, computed from metering data. |\n45\t| HLR-2 | P1 | E002 (bundled) — CSV export and the stable API are export modes of the same report surface over the same metering data; a split would build the same data structure and API endpoint twice and add an artificial serialization edge, so the P1 stays bundled with the P0 report work. | |\n46\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E003 | Dashboard admin view: current-month consumption against each project's Kueue quota. |\n47\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E004 | Documentation of report semantics and export integration. Docs-authoring priority derived from strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception. |\n48\t\n49\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n50\t\n51\t## Health Warnings\n52\t\n53\t1. **Missing strategy sections.** The strategy provides no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, no Non-Functional Requirements, and no per-component change descriptions in the Affected Components list. Team assignments were inferred from `.context/architecture-context/` owning teams (RHOAI Dashboard for the Dashboard component, Distributed Workloads for the Distributed Workloads component). The documentation epic is assigned to a docs team (\"RHOAI Docs\") as an assumption — no team information exists in the strategy; verify before scheduling.\n54\t2. **External timing risk (not an AC).** The metering data schema finalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands. Tier 2 work (E002, E003, E004) is precondition-gated on that data becoming available; this is a schedule risk recorded in epic Risks/Notes, not an acceptance criterion. E001 is the Tier 1 risk-reduction activity that pins the consumption contract early.\n55\t3. **Dashboard consumption interface.** The Dashboard architecture context states the Dashboard talks to the Kubernetes API only and owns no data plane, while the strategy mandates a stable report API for finance tooling. Whether the Dashboard admin view consumes report data via that API or via a Kubernetes surface is an implementation decision for the Dashboard team; it does not change the epic structure. Flagged for the implementing team.\n56\t4. **Priority note.** HLR-2 (P1) is deliberately bundled into the P0 report epic (see traceability matrix row for the rationale). No other priority inversions detected.\n57\t\n58\t## Tiered Delivery\n59\t\n60\tThe external dependency (RHAISTRAT-9990 metering data, uncertain landing timing) admits a tiered split:\n61\t\n62\t- **Tier 1 (independent, risk-reduction):** E001 — the metering data consumption contract Investigation. It can start immediately against the in-flight schema work, de-risks the report implementation, and does not require RHAISTRAT-9990 to have landed.\n63\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated):** E002, E003, E004 — all report computation, Dashboard presentation, and documentation work that consumes the metering data. These are gated by E001's contract and, as a schedule precondition, by RHAISTRAT-9990 delivering the metering data.\n64\t\n65\tNo separate epic is created for the external dependency itself (Rule 21, third party resolves): it is modeled as a precondition with tiered delivery.\n66\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chat